WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.800
The Jacobian.

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.533
One slope is not enough when a function takes a vector in and gives a vector out.

3
00:00:07.733 --> 00:00:12.667
If a function maps n inputs to m outputs, no single number describes its slope.

4
00:00:12.967 --> 00:00:20.333
The Jacobian is an m by n matrix holding every partial derivative of every output with respect to every input.

5
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:24.333
Row i answers: how does output i respond to each input?

6
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:28.767
Take a layer that maps sixty four features to thirty two.

7
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:30.733
Sixty four numbers go in.

8
00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:32.288
Thirty two come out.

9
00:00:32.288 --> 00:00:35.800
Its Jacobian has thirty two rows and sixty four columns.

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.833
Here is a real example, turning an orbit's radius and angle into x and y.

11
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:45.500
Two inputs, two outputs, so a two by two Jacobian.

12
00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:50.067
The first column says how x and y respond to a change in radius.

13
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:53.633
The second says how they respond to a change in angle.

14
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:56.367
We can build it by nudging.

15
00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:00.233
This function turns polar orbit coordinates into x and y.

16
00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:05.033
Nudge the radius, and the change in both outputs becomes the first column.

17
00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:08.059
Nudge the angle, and we get the second column.

18
00:01:08.059 --> 00:01:10.133
It matches the formula from before.

19
00:01:11.333 --> 00:01:16.500
The Jacobian is also the best linear approximation of a function near a point.

20
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.733
So you can push uncertainty through a coordinate change.

21
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:26.367
Sandwich the old uncertainty between J and its transpose, and you get the uncertainty in x and y.

22
00:01:27.567 --> 00:01:29.000
Here is where it shows up.

23
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:33.633
An asteroid's position arrives as range and angle, with measurement error.

24
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:37.933
To report error bars on a map, propagate them through the Jacobian.

25
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:43.400
And inside PyTorch, every layer's backward pass is a Jacobian times a vector.

26
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:45.393
Let's recap.

27
00:01:45.393 --> 00:01:48.500
Vector in, vector out needs a matrix of slopes.

28
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.733
Its shape is outputs by inputs.

29
00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:53.533
It is the best local linear approximation.

30
00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:56.412
And backprop chains Jacobians together.

31
00:01:56.412 --> 00:01:57.800
Next up: the Hessian.
